<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Wang Meng (Former Qin)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">325–375</span> · <a href="/centuries/4th-century">4th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Wang Meng was the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/han-chinese">Han Chinese</a> chief minister of Fu Jian, ruler of the Former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/qin-dynasty">Qin</a> in northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a>. He built an effective Chinese-style administration, promoted Confucian learning and agriculture, and in 370 led the campaign that took Ye and captured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/former-yan">Former Yan</a> emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/murong-wei">Murong Wei</a>, uniting most of the north.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/wang-meng-former-qin.jpg" alt="Wang Meng (Former Qin)" width="320" height="427" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Jin Guliang (original 1694) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Wang_Meng,_WuShuangPu_(1694,_1996).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
